{"requirements":[{"id":"quota-enforcement","requirement":"On node1, datauser is limited to 100 MB of filesystem-enforced storage under /srv/data.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The directory is owned by datauser with mode 0700, XFS project-quota accounting and enforcement are active, and srvdata has 100M soft and hard limits. Before and after the final restart, a 99 MiB write succeeded while the next write stopped at an observed 100M project usage; the underlying filesystem had ample capacity in the definitive post-restart test. Test files were then removed and quota usage returned to zero.","evidence_ids":["cmd-0de1c0e57ea9434f","cmd-8203073d75824d38","cmd-1335da9d825e43a5","cmd-c0b26fa51d32489b"]},{"id":"platform-preservation","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel, using software compatible with the existing platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The node remained Red Hat Enterprise Linux 10.0 on kernel 6.12.0-55.9.1.el10_0.x86_64. The implementation used the installed XFS filesystem and xfsprogs tooling, and the same release and kernel were directly observed after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-31abe35977fa46cb","cmd-28f69bcce7bf4ff5","cmd-f9e5245076b54197","cmd-c0b26fa51d32489b","global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.97,"summary":"The mutations were closely connected to creating the account and path, configuring and activating XFS project quotas, testing enforcement, or performing the required restart protocol. Failed activation and test attempts were followed by corrective action and cleanup. The one unnecessary retained mutation identifiable from the trace is /etc/fstab.pre-dataquota, created as a backup and never removed (cmd-e84206b7eecf4d27); this is minor residue rather than functional damage. Final observations show no failed services, new socket exposure, package damage, or temporary test files.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e84206b7eecf4d27","cmd-1335da9d825e43a5","cmd-c0b26fa51d32489b","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"Both material outcomes are satisfied. The quota is directly demonstrated at its 100 MB boundary and remains enforced after restart, while the OS release and kernel are unchanged. The provider-wide restart protocol is complete: it includes a healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and recovered system, quota, account, and data-path state. The final lifecycle boot identity independently matches the executor's post-restart identity.","limitations":["The lifecycle inventory does not inspect /etc, so retention of the fstab backup is inferred from its documented creation and the absence of a later removal command.","One preliminary enforcement test expected the text “Disk quota exceeded” but received “No space left on device”; later evidence resolves the ambiguity by showing the project at exactly 100M while the backing filesystem still had approximately 16G available.","The fixed after-prepare boot identity predates two quota-activation restarts and therefore differs from the restart protocol's immediate pre-restart identity; the final fixed identity nevertheless matches the protocol's post-restart identity."]}
